Saint Lucia Population and Housing Census - 2022
Weighting Procedure
One well-known aspect of censuses is that they are prone to both overcounts and undercounts.
Overcounts occur when households or individuals are counted more than once; a common example is
when enumerators inadvertently cross into their colleague's assigned areas. Undercount is more
common in enumeration exercises and occurs when households are missed or are not counted for
several reasons. A common example is when individuals refuse to be included in the census, forcing
the enumerator to rely on last-resort information. Countries estimate undercounts through post-
enumeration triangulation exercises, canvassing a sample of districts thoroughly to enhance accuracy.
These results are compared with regular census data to assess the coverage error. Such exercises are
cost-prohibitive and often unfeasible for island states.
Mapping and listing operations conducted by qualified statisticians often result in more comprehensive
household data coverage when compared to data from census enumeration exercises.
This is attributed to the systematic and meticulous nature of these operations which typically involve
direct observation and verification by these skilled professionals (Table 1). Studies, including those by
the U.S. Census Bureau, support this approach by showing improved data quality and coverage when
geospatial operations are utilized during census processes (Census.gov).
Table 1: Difference Between Mapping Listing and Census Proper Data
Operation Number of Number of Number of Occupied Number of
Buildings Inhabited Buildings Dwellings Households
Mapping/listing 73,665 61,299 65,939 60,244
Census Process 73,727 47,025 51,602 50,5882
Table 1 shows both operations recorded approximately the same number of buildings. The census
enumeration recorded fewer dwellings, households, and inhabited buildings than the electronic
mapping exercise. Since the mapping and listing team can only observe dwellings externally, the
records of inhabited buildings are unlikely to match those reported by enumerators. Consequently, the
mapping and listing data were used to estimate the undercount. As a result of the undercount in Table
1, weighted factors were calculated in Table 2 and applied to generate the population and housing
statistics.
